<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Totonac state of Cempoala</h1><p class="st-meta">1200 CE – 1521 CE (approx.) · capital Cempoala</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The chief city of the Totonacs on the Gulf Coast of Veracruz, whose ruler became the first indigenous ally of Hernán Cortés in 1519.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/totonacs">Totonacs</a> dominated central Veracruz in the Postclassic period, and Cempoala (Zempoala) became their principal city from around 1200, after the decline of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/el-tajin">El Tajín</a>. It lay on a fertile coastal plain near the Actopan river.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Cempoala was a large city of walled ceremonial precincts with temples built of river stones and stucco, surrounded by irrigated fields and gardens. It was ruled by a lord whom Spanish chroniclers called the 'Fat Cacique'. In the mid-15th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/totonacs">Totonac</a> region came under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a>, which demanded heavy tribute.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1519 Cortés landed nearby and persuaded Cempoala to rebel against Aztec tax collectors; its warriors joined his march inland. In 1520 Cortés defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panfilo-de-narvaez">Pánfilo de Narváez</a> at Cempoala. Epidemics soon devastated the city, which was largely deserted by the late 16th century.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 1200 CE – 1521 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:83.58%;width:6.39%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1200 CE – 1521 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th